{
  "image_name": "2c32b5b2453785d72de982a96e6493ba.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sensory neurons in the dorsal root ganglion and spinal cord can contribute to abnormal pain signaling. On the left, a schematic shows primary sensory fibers running from the peripheral nerve through the dorsal root ganglion into the spinal cord, highlighting that changes can occur both in the nerve endings and in the neuron cell bodies. A magnified view of the ganglion emphasizes different sensory neuron classes, including nociceptors, mechanoreceptors, and proprioceptors, suggesting that several types of afferents may be involved.\n\nThe right side organizes key mechanisms that can make these neurons pathological. These include peripheral sensitization, where sensory endings become more responsive; ectopic neuronal activity, meaning abnormal spontaneous firing in fibers or cell bodies; and gene dysregulation, which appears to alter channel expression or function and thereby sensitize neurons. The diagram also shows presynaptic modulation, indicating enhanced neurotransmitter release from nociceptors, and a switch of sensory modality, where neurons seem to acquire new signaling roles, such as normally touch-sensitive fibers contributing to pain. Overall, the figure presents neuropathic or chronic pain as arising from multiple interacting changes along the sensory pathway rather than from a single lesion site.",
